records.claim_root_cause_or_alert:
In HttpObjectDecoder.splitHeader, the parser scans for the end of the header field name (stopping at whitespace or a colon) and then independently scans forward for the colon delimiter:
  for (colonEnd = nameEnd; colonEnd < length; colonEnd ++) {
      if (sb.charAt(colonEnd) == ':') { colonEnd ++; break; }
  }
This loop silently skips any whitespace between the header name and the colon without checking whether colonEnd == nameEnd. According to RFC 7230 §3.2.4, no whitespace is permitted between a header field-name and the colon, and a server MUST reject such requests with 400 Bad Request. Netty instead extracts the trimmed header name (e.g., 'Transfer-Encoding' from 'Transfer-Encoding : chunked') and adds it to the message headers. Furthermore, if no colon is present at all, the parser accepts the line with an empty value instead of rejecting the malformed header. In readHeaders, multiple Content-Length headers or contradictory Content-Length and Transfer-Encoding headers are also processed without rejecting the ambiguous request.

